  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Tanned Toothcrust (Hydnomerulius pinastri) is a small, crust-like fungus characterized by its tooth-like or spine-like structures on the underside rather than gills or pores. This wood-decay fungus is commonly found on coniferous wood throughout New England and plays an important ecological role in forest decomposition.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Spine-like Structures",
      "description": "The underside features distinctive small, tooth-like spines or ridges rather than gills, which immediately distinguishes it from other crust fungi",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Tan to Brown Color",
      "description": "The fruiting body displays a characteristic tan, beige, or light brown coloration that becomes more prominent with age",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Substrate Preference",
      "description": "Found exclusively or nearly exclusively on decaying conifer wood, particularly pine and spruce, which aids in identification",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Tanned Toothcrust grows on dead or dying coniferous wood, especially pine and spruce logs and branches, in forests throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike many fungi that fruit seasonally, Hydnomerulius pinastri can persist year-round on wood, making it visible even in winter when most other fungi are dormant.",
